Commit: ad661034ac7f7f39c86a87b0c82c0bcc68edb993

We currently use 
```ts
    rewrites: () => [
        {
            source: '/custom-asset-prefix/:path*',
            destination: '/:path*',
        },
    ],
```
as a workaround, but isn't ideal

> Hmm sounds like both should be used for this case

I believe @leerob's intention with https://github.com/vercel/next.js/pull/55927#discussion_r1335320865 was to remove `disabled` as it hides buttons in some assistive technologies. I hope Lee can correct me if I am misunderstanding. To extend on Lee's point, `disabled={true}` also removes the button from the tab order, which can cause frustration for people using keyboard input as well.

Using both attributes makes `aria-disabled` redundant, as `aria-disabled` only conveys semantics while `disabled` does that and more. This would break the first rule of ARIA. So we could keep the example simple, and only use one, or we could nudge the reader to use `useFormStatus` to make an accessible button that prevents duplicate results ([reference for design pattern](https://www.smashingmagazine.com/2021/08/frustrating-design-patterns-disabled-buttons/#making-disabled-buttons-more-inclusive)).

Here is an example of what that could look (untested):

```jsx
'use client'

import { useFormStatus } from 'react-dom'

export function SubmitButton() {
  const { pending } = useFormStatus()

  const handleClick = (event) => {
    if (pending) {
      event.preventDefault()
    }
  }

  return (
    <button type="submit" aria-disabled={pending} onClick={handleClick}>
      Add
    </button>
  )
}
```

Suggestion:

```ts
const obs = window.PerformanceObserver && new PerformanceObserver((list) => {
  const entry = list.getEntries()[0]
  gcEvents.push(entry)

  if (entry.duration > LONG_RUNNING_GC_THRESHOLD_MS) {
    warn(bold(`Long running GC detected: ${entry.duration.toFixed(2)}ms`))
  }
})

/**
 * Starts recording garbage collection events in the process and warn on long
 * running GCs. To disable, call `stopObservingGc`.
 */
export function startObservingGc() {
  obs?.observe({ entryTypes: ['gc'] })
}

export function stopObservingGc() {
  obs?.disconnect()
}
```

