Commit: 5ae164c08bbc0690f613162a45e7790a0bed1207

The documentation of `@mdx-js/react` is not a spec. It just describes the API of `@mdx-js/react`. Its `useMDXComponents` accepts an *optional* argument, which is only passed if it’s called by `<MDXProvider />`.

If you compile MDX, you see `useMDXComponents` really doesn’t receive any arguments. Just look at the example of `providerImportSource` on https://mdxjs.com/packages/mdx/#fields (Or better, look at [our tests](https://github.com/mdx-js/mdx/blob/51500e2be516144c45e2df339b7cba0371abb356/packages/mdx/test/compile.js#L1325-L1345)):

>  ```diff
>  import {Fragment as _Fragment, jsx as _jsx, jsxs as _jsxs} from 'react/jsx-runtime'
>  +import {useMDXComponents as _provideComponents} from '@mdx-js/react'
>
>  export function Thing() {
>    return _jsx(_Fragment, {children: 'World'})
>  }
>
>  function _createMdxContent(props) {
>    const _components = {
>      h1: 'h1',
>  +    ..._provideComponents(),
>      ...props.components
>    }
>    return _jsxs(_components.h1, {children: ['Hello ', _jsx(Thing, {})]})
>  }
>
>  export default function MDXContent(props = {}) {
>  -  const {wrapper: MDXLayout} = props.components || {}
>  +  const {wrapper: MDXLayout} = {
>  +    ..._provideComponents(),
>  +    ...props.components
>  +  }
>
>    return MDXLayout
>      ? _jsx(MDXLayout, {...props, children: _jsx(_createMdxContent, {})})
>      : _createMdxContent()
>  ```

The `components` prop and the provided components are merged using spread syntax. Nothing is passed into `useMDXComponents`.

